.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt PAZARI-AGENT.TOML 5
.Os pazari
.Sh NAME
.Nm pazari-agent.toml
.Nd configuration file for pazari-agent
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr pazari-agent 1
reads its configuration from a TOML file,
.Pa /etc/pazari/agent.toml
by default.
.Ql pazari-agent example-config
prints a complete file with every key at its default.
.Pp
How the agent uses these settings is described in
.Xr pazari-agent 1 .
.Ss Sizes and durations
A
.Ar size
is an integer number of bytes, or a number followed by a unit.
.Cm KiB , MiB , GiB
and
.Cm TiB
are powers of 1024;
.Cm kB , MB , GB
and
.Cm TB
are powers of 1000.
Units are case-insensitive and may be preceded by a space.
.Pp
A
.Ar duration
is a string such as
.Ql 500ms ,
.Ql 5s ,
.Ql 1min
or
.Ql "2s 500ms" .
Units range from
.Cm ns
to
.Cm y .
Unless stated otherwise, a duration must be greater than zero and at most 24
hours.
.Sh SETTINGS
.Ss Top level
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm tick_interval No = Ar duration
How often the agent samples the workload cgroup and the guest's memory.
Default
.Cm 1s .
.Pp
A throttle wakes the agent immediately, so a shorter tick does not make it ask
for memory sooner.
It does make the agent notice sooner that granted memory has arrived, and retry
sooner after a refusal.
Below
.Cm runtime.hotplug_debounce ,
the tick is the faster way the agent learns its memory has changed.
.El
.Ss [engine]
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm service_reserve No = Ar size
Memory kept below
.Va memory.max
for everything in the guest other than the workload.
Required; may be 0.
.Pp
This only lowers the workload's limit.
To also protect the guest's services from reclaim, set
.Va MemoryMin
on
.Pa system.slice .
.It Cm burst_reserve No = Ar size
The gap between
.Va memory.high
and
.Va memory.max .
Required; must be greater than 0.
See
.Sx SIZING .
.It Cm grow_step No = Ar size
The minimum a grant request asks for.
Default
.Cm 512MiB .
.Pp
Requests are rounded up to the guest's memory block size, 128MiB on x86-64, so
a smaller step behaves as one block.
.It Cm escalate_within No = Ar duration
If the workload is throttled again within this long of a grow completing, the
next request's step doubles, up to 64 times
.Cm grow_step .
Default
.Cm 10s .
.It Cm deny_backoff No = Ar duration
How long to wait before retrying a refused request, when the host does not
specify a delay.
Default
.Cm 5s .
.It Cm settle_grace No = Ar duration
How long to wait for granted memory to arrive before accepting the size the
guest has.
Default
.Cm 10s .
This only matters when a resize arrives short of its target.
.It Cm release_intervals No = Ar count
How many consecutive ticks the guest must have
.Cm shrink_step
to spare, beyond both reserves, before memory is released.
Measured from
.Va MemAvailable .
Default
.Cm 3 .
.Pp
The wait is this multiplied by
.Cm tick_interval ,
so changing the tick changes it too.
Too short a wait releases memory between bursts of a workload that will want it
back.
.It Cm shrink_step No = Ar size
How much one release returns.
Default
.Cm 256MiB .
.It Cm shrink_cooldown No = Ar duration
Minimum time between releases.
May be 0.
Default
.Cm 30s .
.Pp
Released memory is reclaimed from the workload first, and any of it the workload
still uses must be faulted back in.
A workload whose usage rises and falls needs a cooldown longer than its cycle.
.It Cm reclaim_swappiness No = Ar integer
The
.Va swappiness
passed to each
.Va memory.reclaim
write, from 0 to 200.
If unset, the guest's
.Va vm.swappiness
applies.
.Pp
At 0, releases take only page cache, never anonymous memory.
A guest with no reclaimable page cache then has nothing to release.
.El
.Ss [engine.exhaustion]
What the agent does when the guest reaches its ceiling.
Optional; defaults to
.Cm stall
with a
.Cm 60s
timeout.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm kind No = Qq Cm stall
Keep the workload throttled.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm timeout No = Ar duration
How long to keep the workload throttled before releasing the brake and letting
it reach
.Va memory.max .
Default
.Cm 60s .
.Pp
Use a long timeout when a stalled workload is still useful, for example while
an operator raises the ceiling.
.El
.It Cm kind No = Qq Cm kill
Release the brake immediately, and have the kernel kill the whole cgroup when
it reaches
.Va memory.max .
.El
.Ss [runtime]
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm cgroup No = Ar path
Absolute path of the workload cgroup, for example
.Pa /sys/fs/cgroup/workload.slice .
Required.
.Pp
The agent only sees memory charged to this cgroup.
See
.Xr pazari 7
for placing containers in it.
.It Cm hotplug_debounce No = Ar duration
After a memory hot-plug event, how long to wait for further events before
re-reading the guest's memory.
A resize raises one event per memory block.
Default
.Cm 250ms .
.It Cm reclaim_chunk No = Ar size
The largest single
.Va memory.reclaim
write.
Larger chunks reclaim faster but stall the workload longer per write.
Default
.Cm 64MiB .
.It Cm thp_always_fatal No = Ar boolean
Whether
.Va transparent_hugepage=always
fails preflight rather than warning.
Default
.Cm true .
.Pp
Under
.Cm always ,
a single fault can take 2MiB instead of 4KiB, so the workload crosses the burst
reserve far faster than it was sized for.
Boot the guest with
.Va transparent_hugepage=madvise .
Set this to
.Cm false
only if the guest cannot be rebooted.
.El
.Ss [runtime.backend]
How the agent sets
.Va memory.high
and
.Va memory.max .
Other cgroup files are always written directly.
Optional; defaults to
.Cm cgroupfs .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm kind No = Qq Cm cgroupfs
Write the cgroup files directly.
.It Cm kind No = Qq Cm systemd
Set
.Va MemoryHigh
and
.Va MemoryMax
as runtime properties of a unit over D-Bus, so that a
.Ql systemctl daemon-reload
does not reset them.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm unit No = Ar name
The unit, for example
.Ql workload.slice .
Required.
.El
.El
.Ss [metrics]
Optional.
Without it, the agent serves no metrics.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm listen No = Ar address
Address to serve OpenMetrics on, for example
.Ql 127.0.0.1:9651 .
Required.
Any
.Cm GET
request is answered with the metrics; other methods get 405.
.El
.Ss [transport]
How the agent reaches
.Xr pazarid 1 .
Required.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm kind No = Qq Cm vsock
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm cid No = Ar integer
The host's context ID.
Default
.Cm 2 .
.It Cm port No = Ar integer
Default
.Cm 4650 .
.El
.It Cm kind No = Qq Cm unix
For guests that are not virtual machines.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm path No = Ar path
The daemon's socket.
Required.
.El
.El
.Pp
Both kinds accept:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm handshake_timeout No = Ar duration
How long to wait for the daemon to answer a new connection.
Default
.Cm 10s .
.It Cm reconnect_min No = Ar duration
First reconnect delay.
Default
.Cm 500ms .
.It Cm reconnect_max No = Ar duration
Maximum reconnect delay; the delay doubles up to this.
Must be at least
.Cm reconnect_min .
Default
.Cm 30s .
.Pp
The agent keeps its current limits while disconnected, so this is also how long
a guest may keep stale limits after the host returns.
.El
.Sh SIZING
.Ss service_reserve
With the workload stopped, read
.Va memory.current
of
.Pa system.slice
and anything else outside the workload cgroup, and add a margin.
Too little lets a throttled workload squeeze the agent itself; too much wastes
granted memory.
.Ss burst_reserve
The reserve must meet two conditions.
.Pp
First, it must hold what the workload allocates during one grow round trip:
the round trip on your host multiplied by the workload's unthrottled allocation
rate.
At 1GiB/s and 300ms, that is 300MiB.
It must also hold the workload's largest single allocation, since the kernel
throttles after an allocation, not during it.
.Pp
Second, it must be wider than the point at which the kernel's throttle stops the
workload altogether.
The throttle delay grows with the overshoot past
.Va memory.high
and saturates at about two seconds per allocation.
A reserve wider than that point holds a refused workload indefinitely; a
narrower one only slows it on its way to
.Va memory.max .
To find the point, lower
.Cm burst_reserve
until a workload that is refused memory starts reaching
.Va memory.max ,
visible as
.Va pazari_agent_max_events
increasing, and use several times that value.
.Pp
Measure again after changing the transparent hugepage mode, adding or removing
swap, or changing how the workload allocates.
.Ss Reference measurements
Taken with
.Xr pazari-stress 1
in a guest without swap, faulting anonymous memory one page at a time as fast
as the kernel allowed.
Measure on your own hardware.
.Pp
Grow round trip, from request to
.Va MemTotal
rising, over 11 grows:
.Bl -column "tick_interval" "median" "95th percentile" -offset indent
.It Sy tick_interval Ta Sy median Ta Sy 95th percentile
.It 500ms Ta 302ms Ta 402ms
.It 100ms Ta 118ms Ta 180ms
.El
.Pp
Time for a refused workload to reach
.Va memory.max ,
over two runs of 360 seconds:
.Bl -column "burst_reserve" "never; stopped 14\-18MiB into the reserve" -offset indent
.It Sy burst_reserve Ta Sy result
.It none Ta under 5s
.It 16MiB Ta 11s and 35s
.It 64MiB Ta never; stopped 17\-18MiB into the reserve
.It 256MiB Ta never; stopped 14\-15MiB into the reserve
.El
.Ss Usage, not commit
Size reserves from
.Va memory.current ,
not
.Va Committed_AS .
Mapped but untouched memory counts towards commit but not usage, and the agent
sees only usage.
.Sh METRICS
With
.Cm [metrics]
configured, the agent publishes the following, each prefixed
.Ql pazari_agent_ .
.Ss Gauges
.Bl -tag -width "workload_usage_bytes" -compact
.It Va mode
1 for the current mode, 0 for the others, labelled
.Va mode .
.It Va memory_high_bytes
.Va memory.high
as last written.
.It Va memory_max_bytes
.Va memory.max
as last written.
.It Va brake_released
1 while
.Va memory.high
equals
.Va memory.max .
.It Va total_mem_bytes
The guest's
.Va MemTotal .
.It Va ceiling_bytes
The ceiling set by the host, or 0 if not yet known.
.It Va workload_usage_bytes
.Va memory.current
of the workload cgroup.
.It Va swap_used_bytes
Swap used by the workload cgroup.
.It Va high_events
The cgroup's
.Va high
event count.
.It Va max_events
The cgroup's
.Va max
event count.
Should stay at 0.
.It Va psi_some10
The workload's memory pressure.
.It Va connected
1 while connected to the host.
.El
.Ss Counters
.Bl -tag -width "reclaim_asked_bytes_total" -compact
.It Va throttle_wakes_total
Times the agent was woken by a throttle.
.It Va grant_requests_total
Grant requests sent.
.It Va grants_total
Grants received.
.It Va denies_total
Refusals received, labelled
.Va reason .
.It Va offers_total
Offers to release sent.
.It Va reclaims_total
Reclaim requests received from the host.
.It Va reclaim_asked_bytes_total
Bytes those requests asked for.
.It Va exhaustions_total
Times the agent became exhausted.
.It Va connections_total
Connections established.
.It Va disconnections_total
Connections lost.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/pazari/agent.toml
Default configuration file.
.It Pa share/doc/pazari/examples/agent.toml
Annotated example, installed with the package.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A guest that boots with 2GiB, keeps 512MiB for its own services, uses a 256MiB
burst reserve, and stalls for up to five minutes when exhausted:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tick_interval = "500ms"

[engine]
service_reserve = "512MiB"
burst_reserve = "256MiB"

[engine.exhaustion]
kind = "stall"
timeout = "300s"

[runtime]
cgroup = "/sys/fs/cgroup/workload.slice"

[runtime.backend]
kind = "systemd"
unit = "workload.slice"

[metrics]
listen = "127.0.0.1:9651"

[transport]
kind = "vsock"
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pazari-agent 1 ,
.Xr pazari-stress 1 ,
.Xr pazarid 1 ,
